---
import Base from '../../../../layouts/Base.astro';

const SITE = 'https://precisionsolutionstech.com';
const title = 'Assay Support | Precision Solutions Tech';
const description =
  'Support for Assay, the Mac app that opens any file and converts it with a check of what was kept: requirements, Assay Unlock, the Finder menu and Quick Look previews, and answers to common questions.';

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: `${SITE}/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Apps', item: `${SITE}/apps/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Assay', item: `${SITE}/apps/assay/` },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 4, name: 'Support', item: `${SITE}/apps/assay/support/` },
    ],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={title}
  description={description}
  path="/apps/assay/support/"
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  active="apps"
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <ol>
        <li><a href="/">Home</a></li>
        <li><a href="/apps/">Apps</a></li>
        <li><a href="/apps/assay/">Assay</a></li>
        <li>Support</li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <article class="prose">
      <h1>Assay Support</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Something not working, or a question the app does not answer? Email
        <a href="mailto:support@precisionsolutionstech.com">support@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
        and tell us your macOS version, the kind of file (its extension is enough — you do not need to
        send the file), and what you expected to happen. We read every message.
      </p>

      <h2 id="requirements">Requirements</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>macOS 14 (Sonoma) or later</li>
        <li>Apple Silicon or Intel — Assay is native on both</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="unlock">Free, and Assay Unlock</h2>
      <p>
        Assay is free to download, and viewing is free for good: every file opens, every viewer
        works, and the inspector shows exactly what a conversion would keep and what it would lose.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Assay Unlock</strong> is a one-time in-app purchase that adds converting, editing and
        saving, and trimming and exporting sound and video. It is not a subscription. Buy it from the
        sheet that appears when you press Convert or Edit, or from <strong>Assay → Unlock
        Assay…</strong>.
      </p>
      <h3>I bought it, but Assay is asking again</h3>
      <p>
        Choose <strong>Assay → Restore Purchase</strong>, or Restore Purchase on the unlock sheet. The
        purchase belongs to your Apple ID, so it works on every Mac signed in to the App Store with
        that Apple ID. If you used Ask to Buy, Assay unlocks by itself once the purchase is approved.
      </p>
      <h3>Refunds</h3>
      <p>
        Purchases are made through the Mac App Store, so refunds are handled by Apple at
        <a href="https://reportaproblem.apple.com">reportaproblem.apple.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="finder">The Finder menu and Quick Look previews</h2>
      <p>
        Assay adds <strong>Open in Assay</strong> and <strong>Convert with Assay…</strong> to
        Finder’s right-click menu, and space-bar previews for files macOS cannot show on its own — dBASE
        tables, SQLite databases, Outlook <code>.msg</code> messages, TOML, JSON Lines and more.
      </p>
      <p>
        macOS asks you to switch extensions on yourself. Choose <strong>Assay → Turn On Finder Menu and
        Previews…</strong>. That opens the place in System Settings where extensions are switched on —
        <strong>General → Login Items &amp; Extensions</strong> on macOS 15 and later,
        <strong>Privacy &amp; Security → Extensions</strong> on macOS 14 — where you turn on Assay
        under <em>Finder</em> and <em>Quick Look</em>. If the
        menu still does not appear, relaunch Finder (hold ⌥, right-click Finder in the Dock, choose
        Relaunch).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Convert with Assay…</strong> opens the file in Assay with what the conversion would
        keep and lose already showing. Without Assay Unlock you can read that report; converting asks
        for the unlock.
      </p>

      <h2 id="troubleshooting">Common questions</h2>
      <h3>Assay says my file was “named wrongly”</h3>
      <p>
        Assay identifies files by their contents, not their extension. A spreadsheet saved as
        <code>.csv</code> that is really an Excel workbook, or a <code>.doc</code> that is really a web
        page, is reported as what it actually is.
      </p>
      <h3>Why does a conversion say something will be lost?</h3>
      <p>
        Every conversion is checked: Assay writes the result, reads it back, and compares it with the
        original. When a format cannot hold something — leading zeros in a spreadsheet opened by Excel,
        a table wider than Word allows, transparency in a JPEG — Assay says so, counts it, and tells you
        which format would keep it. “Nothing lost” is only said when the result was read back and
        matched.
      </p>
      <h3>Why can’t I convert a PDF to Word?</h3>
      <p>
        A PDF is a drawing of pages: text and pictures placed at positions, with no paragraphs, headings
        or reliable fonts to rebuild a document from. A Word file made from one would not look like the
        original, so Assay does not offer it. It converts a PDF to its words as Plain Text, or to
        pictures of its pages as PNG, JPEG or TIFF, which look exactly like it.
      </p>
      <h3>A dBASE table’s memo fields are empty</h3>
      <p>
        dBASE keeps memo text in a separate <code>.fpt</code> or <code>.dbt</code> file with the same
        name, beside the table. Make sure that file is in the same folder as the <code>.dbf</code>, then
        open the table again.
      </p>
      <h3>An email or web page shows “not loaded”</h3>
      <p>
        Assay holds back anything a file uses from the web until you ask for it, which also stops
        tracking pixels from telling the sender you opened an email. Choose <em>Load Remote Images</em>
        or <em>Load Remote Content</em> above it to load it for that file.
      </p>
      <h3>A page’s pictures show as missing</h3>
      <p>
        macOS lets Assay read only the file you opened, not the folder around it. Choose the
        <em>Allow…</em> button above the page and pick its folder; Assay remembers it. Assay › Forget
        Folders Allowed for Pictures takes every folder back.
      </p>
      <h3>Converting a web page</h3>
      <p>
        Assay converts a page as your browser shows it: its stylesheets, fonts and pictures. PDF is the
        page exactly as printed; Word carries the fonts, colours, alignment and pictures; HTML becomes one
        self-contained file. If the page uses things from the web you have not loaded, Assay asks whether
        to load them into the converted file.
      </p>
      <h3>Can Assay convert a whole folder?</h3>
      <p>Assay works on one file at a time.</p>

      <h2 id="privacy">Privacy</h2>
      <p>
        Assay runs entirely on your Mac. Your files are opened, converted and previewed locally and are
        never sent to us. Assay goes on the internet only to load a file’s remote content when you ask
        it to, and never to us. There is no account and no analytics. Full detail is in the <a href="/legal/privacy/assay/">Assay Privacy Policy</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="contact">Contact</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="mailto:support@precisionsolutionstech.com">support@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
        ·
        <a href="mailto:hello@precisionsolutionstech.com">hello@precisionsolutionstech.com</a>
      </p>
      <p>
        <a href="/apps/assay/">Assay overview</a>
        ·
        <a href="/legal/privacy/assay/">Privacy Policy</a>
        ·
        <a href="/about/">About us</a>
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</Base>
